1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:02,530
Go's JSON package has been quietly

2
00:00:02,530 --> 00:00:04,090
hiding bugs for years.

3
00:00:04,090 --> 00:00:06,790
Go 1.27 finally fixes it.

4
00:00:06,840 --> 00:00:09,833
Meet json/v2, now generally available.

5
00:00:09,863 --> 00:00:10,703
Problem one.

6
00:00:10,703 --> 00:00:12,863
The old package matched field names

7
00:00:12,863 --> 00:00:14,183
case insensitively.

8
00:00:14,183 --> 00:00:16,023
So a wrong key could silently

9
00:00:16,023 --> 00:00:16,975
fill your field.

10
00:00:16,975 --> 00:00:19,332
V2 needs an exact match.

11
00:00:19,362 --> 00:00:20,162
Problem two.

12
00:00:20,162 --> 00:00:21,331
Duplicate keys.

13
00:00:21,331 --> 00:00:23,633
V1 silently accepted them.

14
00:00:23,633 --> 00:00:25,500
V2 returns an error.

15
00:00:25,530 --> 00:00:26,410
Problem three.

16
00:00:26,410 --> 00:00:28,330
A nil slice was written as null,

17
00:00:28,330 --> 00:00:29,710
and broke your front end.

18
00:00:29,710 --> 00:00:32,004
V2 writes an empty array.

19
00:00:32,034 --> 00:00:33,154
It's also faster.

20
00:00:33,154 --> 00:00:35,514
Early benchmarks report decoding around

21
00:00:35,514 --> 00:00:36,805
twice as fast.

22
00:00:36,835 --> 00:00:37,595
The best part?

23
00:00:37,595 --> 00:00:39,315
Your old imports still work.

24
00:00:39,315 --> 00:00:41,475
The classic package now runs on

25
00:00:41,475 --> 00:00:43,846
the new engine, with the old behavior.

26
00:00:43,896 --> 00:00:46,536
So upgrade, then move to v2 one package

27
00:00:46,536 --> 00:00:47,296
at a time.

28
00:00:47,296 --> 00:00:49,736
Strict by default beats surprising by

29
00:00:49,736 --> 00:00:50,759
default.

